ted  States for the world  we also make the point that the only  real strategic victory was killing bin  Laden right which we could have done  years earlier it's good that it happened  at all but the one of the many things  that went wrong with the war in Iraq was  the diversion of the effort to rout bin  Laden his troops from Tora Bora and so  that was when he was on the run early it  took another decade to get it right  I mean if I've asked this of panels  before if we had only reinforced the  cockpit doors on airplane right and  killed bin Laden and not done all the  other stuff wouldn't we be in better  shape now we sure would and I think  people would have this kind of  discussion it would be debated in  presidential campaigns as hardly as it  all would be a subject of common  knowledge if the public at large was  connected somehow to the military if you  take everybody who's fought in all the  wars the last dozen years at any time  it's three quarters of one percent of  the population they're twice as many  farm families as there are people people  in the military now I mean the ironic  thing is that when people say well we  should never cut the military of course  they're people who never look into how  much we spend on the military what that  money is going towards really spending  that much makes us weaker we don't have  money to spend on building our nation  right home we don't have the  infrastructure of other countries we  don't have a lot of things other  countries have because we waste 20  percent of our budget on basically  fighting Russia in 1978 it's it's true  and in the you know aircraft what do we  have what 10 aircraft carriers we have  yes we have large you have more aircraft  carriers anybody else the Chinese have  one they bought a used one from Ukraine  that's gonna be a long time before  that's a threat you know politicians  have have known that that the main way  you can do infrastructure or investment  the United States is to call it defense  you know doe-eyed Eisenhower with the  National Defense interstate highway act  and all the DARPA which got the internet  going it would be so much more efficient  with if we could actually invest in  these things in the long run without the  leaky loose eyes attention eyes and air  he's the one who said we have to watch  out for the military-industrial complex  if he made that speech today he would  probably ask to apologize for no Bill  O'Reilly would call him a pinhead if if  anybody goes back to read that farewell  address Eisenhower gave in January 1960  it is incredible because he warns about  things that 50 plus years later are the  reality of having the impact of military  contracting of military shaping foreign  policy that this was something he as a  former commander of the greatest  fighting force in the history of the  world was saying this was a caution to  be this was a caution to observe funny  in his era and and the error that  followed when they were talking about  his era World War two you could make fun  of the military television and movies  were full of funny military people  sergeant bilko Hogan's Heroes  McHale's Navy normil Gomer Pyle right  you could never do those shows today  because it shows military people could  be less than saintly and I think that is  a crucial point that's the sign of a  country comfortable enough with its  military to make fun of it to know that  like any other human institution it was  good and bad and preposterous and Noble  and collectively it was great but it had  a lot of people who weren't great and  now you know it in these twelve years of  war we've removed commanders for sexual  peccadilloes and for leaking to the  press but not for these battlefield  failures that have been so terrible for  the country and for the world all right  Jim thank you so much for being here  thank you very got a chance to read it  [Applause]

